Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

A stuffed VELVETEEN RABBIT sits wedged in the top of a Christmas stocking. He is fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white, with real thread whiskers and ears lined with pink sateen. A sprig of holly rests between his paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

THE BOY, a young child in a nightshirt, yanks the Rabbit from the stocking. He hugs him tight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. And then Aunts and Uncles came to dinner.

The Boy drops the Rabbit onto the floor. He tears into larger parcels.

Tissue paper rustles. A shiny toy engine rolls past. A clockwork mouse whirs. The Velveteen Rabbit lies forgotten against the wall.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight cuts across the toy cupboard. The expensive, mechanical toys stand tall.

MODEL BOAT
My rigging is of the highest technical standard. I've survived two seasons.

TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, puffs out his chest.

TIMOTHY
I am connected with the Government.

The Velveteen Rabbit sits in the shadows, looking down at his soft paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The poor little Rabbit was made to feel very insignificant and commonplace. The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

The SKIN HORSE rests near the nursery fender. He is incredibly old. His brown coat is bald in patches, showing the seams underneath. Most of the hairs in his tail are gone.

The Rabbit hops over and lies beside him.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

The Skin Horse smiles gently.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, looking at his pristine paws.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks later. NANA, a stern woman in an apron, swoops through the room, tossing blocks into a bin.

From down the hall, the Boy cries out.

NANA
Too much trouble to hunt for china dogs at bedtime.

She spots the toy cupboard door open. She marches over, grabs the Velveteen Rabbit by one ear, and drags him out.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Nana marches in and shoves the Rabbit into the Boy's arms.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana turns out the main light, leaving only a night-light burning on the mantelpiece. She exits.

The Boy hugs the Rabbit very tight, rolling over and squishing him under his chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At first he found it rather uncomfortable. But very soon he grew to like it, for the Boy used to talk to him, and made nice tunnels for him under the bedclothes.

The Boy lifts the blankets, creating a dark, warm tent.

THE BOY
This is like the burrows the real rabbits live in.

The Boy drops off to sleep. The Rabbit snuggles down close under his warm chin.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Springtime. The Boy runs across the lawn, pulling a wheelbarrow. The Velveteen Rabbit rides inside it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy. So happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

The Boy builds a fairy hut out of twigs under the raspberry canes. He places the Rabbit inside.

EXT. GARDEN - DUSK

The garden is empty and dark. Dew covers the grass. The Rabbit sits alone by the raspberry canes, wet and earthy.

A candle flame bobs in the dark. Nana approaches, grumbling. She snatches the Rabbit up and rubs him roughly with the corner of her apron.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Nana carries the damp Rabbit into the room. The Boy sits up in bed, stretching out his hands.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes widen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he was happy. The nursery magic had happened to him.

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Summer. Tall bracken ferns sway in the breeze. The Boy sets the Rabbit down in a cozy nest of grass.

THE BOY
Stay here, Bunny. I'm going to play brigands.

The Boy runs off through the trees.

The Rabbit lies alone, watching ants run to and fro between his velvet paws.

The bracken rustles. Two strange beings creep out. They are FURRY RABBIT and WILD RABBIT. They are brand-new, with no seams showing. Their noses twitch.

FURRY RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

Furry Rabbit gives a big hop sideways and stands on his hind legs.

FURRY RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit sits perfectly still. His back is made all in one piece, like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

Wild Rabbit stretches out his neck and stares.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

Wild Rabbit begins to laugh.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

FURRY RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

Furry Rabbit whirls around and dances.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

Furry Rabbit stops. He hops very close, his long whiskers brushing the Velveteen Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and jumps backward.

FURRY RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Heavy footsteps approach. The Boy runs past the bracken.

With a stamp of feet and a flash of white tails, the two wild rabbits disappear.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only the ants move. The Rabbit is all alone.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A lamp burns low. The Boy tosses in bed, his face flushed with fever. He mutters in his sleep.

The Velveteen Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, pressed against the Boy's burning chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a long weary time. The little body was so hot that it burned the Rabbit, but he never stirred. He knew that the Boy needed him.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Bright sunlight streams through wide-open windows. The Boy sits up in a chair on the balcony, wrapped in a shawl, looking at a picture book.

Inside the room, the Velveteen Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes. His fur is rubbed away, his pink nose faded to grey.

THE DOCTOR stands with Nana near the bed.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

THE DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

NANA
But he loves it so.

THE DOCTOR
Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

Nana grabs a rough canvas sack. She sweeps the old picture books and the Velveteen Rabbit into it.

EXT. BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The sack sits on the dirt near a thicket of raspberry canes. The top is untied.

The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles his head through the opening. He shivers in the cold night air. His threadbare coat offers no protection.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden. The quiet evenings in the wood. The wonderful day when he first knew that he was Real.

The Rabbit looks toward the dark house.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A single tear, a real tear, trickles down his shabby velvet nose. It falls to the ground.

Where the tear strikes the earth, a mysterious flower sprouts. It has slender emerald leaves and a blossom like a golden cup.

The blossom opens. Out steps a FAIRY. Her dress is made of pearl and dew-drops. Flowers weave through her hair.

She steps close to the Rabbit, gathers him up in her arms, and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The Rabbit stares at her beautiful face.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She holds him close and takes flight.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

The moon shines bright over the forest. The fronds of the bracken shine like frosted silver.

Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass.

The Fairy lands in the open glade. The wild rabbits stop dancing and form a ring around her.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit one last time and sets him down on the grass.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits quite still. He looks at the wild rabbits, remembering his missing hind legs. He doesn't want to move.

Something tickles his nose.

Without thinking, he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He looks down. Instead of dingy velveteen, he has brown fur, soft and shiny. His ears twitch by themselves. His whiskers brush the grass.

He gives one leap. He springs about the turf, jumping sideways and whirling around.

He stops to look for the Fairy, but she is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Springtime. The Boy plays in the dirt behind the house.

Two rabbits creep out from the bracken and peek at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted.

The spotted rabbit stares at the Boy with round black eyes.

THE BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit twitches his nose, then turns and bounds joyfully into the deep forest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.
